            "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7839969",
            "section": "Metric(s)",
            "local_protocol": "_collection/miccai_gapfill_20261024/MICCAI-2023-R10/CerebralarterysegmentationChallenge_04-18-2023_10-38-11.pdf",
            "registered_task_index": 1
          },
          "ranking_reference": {
            "source_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7839969",
            "section": "Ranking method(s)",
            "local_protocol": "_collection/miccai_gapfill_20261024/MICCAI-2023-R10/CerebralarterysegmentationChallenge_04-18-2023_10-38-11.pdf",
            "registered_task_index": 1
          },
          "registered_metrics_text": "a) Define the metric(s) to assess a property of an algorithm. These metrics should reflect the desired algorithm\nproperties described in assessment aim(s) (see above). State which metric(s) were used to compute the ranking(s) (if\nany).\n  • Example 1: Dice Similarity Coefficient (DSC)\n  • Example 2: Area under curve (AUC)\n[1] Dice similarity coefficient.\n[2] Average Hausdorff Distance (AHD).\n[3] Dice similarity coefficient in stenosis region.\n[4] Average Hausdorff Distance (AHD)in stenosis region.\n\n\nAHD, Dice similarity coefficient in stenosis region (DSC_stenosis), and AHD in stenosis region (AHD_stenosis). The\nDSC and AHD scores are computed for all the arteries, while the DSC_stenosis and AHD_stenosis are based on the\nregions of stenosis.\n\n\n\n\fCerebral artery segmentation Challenge\n\nb) Justify why the metric(s) was/were chosen, preferably with reference to the biomedical application.\n\nDice is a common metric to evaluate segmentation performance. AHD is used to assess the goodness of fit of the\nsegmentation to the ground truth boundary and reduce the impact of extreme values. However, simply using\nquantitative metrics alone make it challenging to determine the clinical value of the algorithms, especially when\nthe results are close. We calculated Dice_stenosis and AHD_stenosis separately for the stenosis segment in a more\ncomprehensive evaluation.",
          "registered_ranking_text": "a) Describe the method used to compute a performance rank for all submitted algorithms based on the generated\nmetric results on the test cases. Typically the text will describe how results obtained per case and metric are aggregated\nto arrive at a final score/ranking.\n\n1) Calculate the Dice, AHD, DSC_stenosis, and AHD_stenosis, and Running time for all cases.\n2) Rank the Dice, AHD, DSC_stenosis, and AHD_stenosis.\n3) Calculate the final score and use it to determine the final ranking. Final Score = 35%×DSC + 35%×AHD +\n15%×DSC_stenosis + 15%×AHD_stenosis.\n\nb) Describe the method(s) used to manage submissions with missing results on test cases.\n\nMissing results (failed segmentation or failed) in the test cases will be given the worst possible score.\n\nc) Justify why the described ranking scheme(s) was/were used.\n\nThis ranking scheme is developed to take two important metrics into account. It provides a balanced scheme to\njudge whether the method can achieve accurate segmentation with better performance simultaneously."
        }
      ],
      "execution_evidence_scope": "Documented submission protocol; not an independent audit of runtime infrastructure."
